**Chapter 2 — The Procedure**
~4,600 words

The trap anatomized — the chapter that does for the classification challenge what Book 3's early chapters did for registry law and Book 5's did for tournament scoring: teaches the reader the machine before the machine moves. Karis, Bracken, and Withrow's counsel take the instrument apart clause by clause across a long night in the records hall, and the chapter walks the findings in order of arrival. It does not allege fraud — Greyvane closed that. It does not contest the assay clause — Vastin's own advisory closed that, and the challenge *cites his advisory approvingly*, which Karis flags as the first genuinely frightening thing in the document: they've stopped arguing with the text. It asserts an evaluation-integrity question and petitions for compelled re-evaluation under standard Arbiter protocol. Bracken supplies the procedure for evaluations that return no valid classification, reading from the registry code in his flattest voice: nullification, suspension, asset-restriction. Withrow supplies what asset-restriction means, from twenty years inside: "Everything with his name on it stops. Records, sponsorship, endorsements, travel. It's expulsion that follows you."

Karis's structural finding is the chapter's spine, delivered with the formality she reserves for findings she doesn't like: the challenge is unpassable *by design without a designer* — the Arbiter protocol simply has no bin for [SHATTERED], and the procedure for no-bin is codified, so the outcome is determined before the first session sits. "It's not a question. It's a corridor. Every door in it is real law, and every door opens one way." And her second finding, said almost in passing, which Chapter 16 will detonate: the assay clause worked for three books because it bypasses Arbiter evaluation entirely — and this instrument's one non-negotiable demand is that Cael stand in front of an Arbiter. "They're not trying to beat the provision. They're trying to move you back inside the instrument. I don't know why that distinction feels important. Noting it."

*Key beat (the first pole, on the page):* the Power Log, end of chapter: "I can work within the system. I have the receipts: a hearing, a provision, a stamp renewed four times, a tournament their own clause put me in. The system has text, and text has held me every time the people failed. This is more text. We'll read it better than they do. We always have." He is wrong, and the book knows exactly how long it will take him to find out.

---

**Chapter 3 — Grounds**
~4,600 words

The defense assembled, and the opposition given its face-that-is-not-a-face. Halcenvane files its response — Karis's research, Bracken's verification, Withrow's name on the cover sheet, the full documentary architecture of Books 3–4 deployed as a standing army — and the registry's answering notice sets the term-length schedule: sessions at the registry seat at Ostrand, evaluation seat empaneled, ruling before term's end. The Compact's counsel of record: **Procurator Jent**. The chapter introduces him in exactly one scene and refuses him a window: a procedural conference, courteous, brief, in which he compliments Karis's filing ("The best-documented enrollment I've reviewed in thirty years. Genuinely.") and then explains, without malice, that the documentation is not in dispute and the petition does not require it to be. Cael, reading him across the table the way he reads everyone, gets the same result the reader gets: competence, courtesy, and nothing behind them but the instruction. The log, after: "Coss wanted to win. This one's been *sent* to win, and the difference is worse. There's no appetite to read. He's a hand. Somebody else is the arm."

Withrow's stake staged honestly: the academy is the named respondent, and defending the enrollment now means Halcenvane spends its post-tournament standing — the standing Withrow built her whole tenure toward — on the continent's most dangerous student. Her scene with Cael is the chapter's quiet center. She does not reassure him. She shows him the ledger, both columns, the way she did in Book 4 Chapter 2, and then: "I read the hearing transcript four times before I ever met you. The law was on your side then and it's on your side now. I'm choosing to keep believing that matters. Make a note of the word *choosing*." Cael makes a note of the word choosing.

*Close on:* the term's geometry laid out — sessions at Ostrand, Lira's Silver bracket opening, Brom's review docketed, the re-evaluation date a blank the proceedings will fill — and the watchers outside the gate, whom Cael counts again and finally places: more senior than last year because the junior work is done. "They're not gathering information anymore," he writes. "They're waiting for a status change."

---

**Chapter 4 — Standing**
~4,600 words

The stakes inventory — the chapter that makes the walkout expensive eighteen chapters early, because a resignation that costs nothing earns nothing. Built as the series' braided-track chapter, one movement per companion, each stake named on the page in its owner's voice.

