The silence is Seln's, and the chapter makes it load-bearing: he attends the whole council and contributes one procedural correction and nothing else. His window, closing the chapter — the longest window Part 2 gives him before the hinge: fifteen years of product, four years of this boy, a locked case that has outlasted nineteen postings, and the sentence he has never permitted himself to finish, forming anyway: *the file's question was always the wrong question — and I am the only person alive holding the file that proves whose question it was.* The window does not open the case. It notes, in Seln's own audit-voice, the cost of the case staying closed: every session the boy fights inside the instrument, believing the instrument is merely broken. The window's last line: "He thinks he's arguing with a malfunction. I've read the maintenance records. Fifteen years I've been deciding to die with that sentence. The deciding has stopped being quiet."

