**Chapter 20 — The Last Bout**
~4,600 words

Ephram's chapter — the ninth fragment, the farewell the Crown yard is owed, and the arc that began with *"I'll wait for the semester evaluation"* completing in the only currency Halcenvane speaks. Ephram's one request, made at the council's end and staged now: a sanctioned bout, full-earnest, before the resignations file — "I waited for the evaluation. Then the tournament. Then a year of watching you hold back in a building that was deciding what you are. I'm done waiting. Before you go — show me straight." Rooke sanctions it without being asked twice and posts it openly, which from Rooke is a eulogy: the Crown yard's last look at the enrollee the brackets could never hold, and the campus knows it and comes.

The bout, full choreography, the series' register: Ephram at cohort-captain grade, Blade doctrine at the speed Rooke built, fighting with the specific ferocity of a man who wants the true measurement or nothing — and Cael answering in kind, the public suite open, nothing held that matters (Shadow sealed still, its logic standing; the log squared this in advance). Four exchanges, the best pure bout the Crown yard has staged since Lira–Brom, and inside the fourth — sustained observation at full earnest, genuine stakes on both sides of it (Ephram fighting for the answer, Cael fighting for the leaving), the conditions the model requires, met on the Daeva precedent and priced in advance per the covenant — the interior action completes. The notice arrives after the stoppage, and the page shows it in full:

```
FRAGMENT ACQUIRED
[unnamed] — Blade-adjacent. Duration: sustained. Integration: partial.
Tier equivalent: Iron.
Note: edge-declaration component; line-reading component.
Contact range.
Acquisition: directed. Engagement: adversarial, earnest — non-hostile.
```

A first, counted on the page: the engagement field has never carried *non-hostile* before — the system's own grammar bending to describe a fight that was entirely earnest and entirely without enmity, which is as close as the architecture has ever come to describing friendship. Cael wins the bout on points and the chapter treats the result as the least important line in it. After, the boards ritual, the series' structure honored one last time on this floor: Ephram, at the rail, without preamble — "Whatever you are, I said I'm glad it's on my side of the floor. The floor's about to be the whole continent. Still glad." He does not learn the mechanism. He does not need to. The log, that night, the covenant's honest accounting: "Nine. Taken in earnest from a friend who asked me to show him straight — so I showed him straight, and the architecture did what it does with earnest. I wrote the price down before the bout. It cost anyway. It always costs. That's how I know I'm still on my side of the line."

*Close on:* the integration's first pull, managed; the resignations drafted on Bracken's counter for morning; and the Crown yard at night, empty, Cael at the rail one last time — the gallery where he charted eleven Paths, the floor where the found family fought each other honestly — counted, catalogued, closed. "Good room," he writes, which from Cael is a whole letter.

---

**Chapter 21 — Resignations**
~4,600 words

The walk-out — the bible's images, staged verbatim, in the order the day would actually run them, and the chapter's discipline is smallness: no speeches, no gate crowd, no institutional convulsion. Paperwork, boxes, and doors. Morning: Bracken's counter. The resignations filed in sequence, each given its exact procedural weight — and **Karis forfeits her ranking** on the page: Iron-tier Rank 3, research standing, archive privileges, the assistant reassigned, each line item read aloud by Bracken because the procedure requires it and signed away in her even hand because she does. Bracken, at the end, off-procedure, the driest grief in the book: "The filing was the best-documented enrollment I have processed in thirty years. It has been a privilege to keep your records." Gault's last panel note, entered the same morning, shown in full: *Assay-provision enrollment: resigned in good standing prior to revocation date. Baseline file: closed complete. The instruments will not see its like.* Midday: **Lira walks out of her last formal-institution class** — mid-session, a Silver-program theory seminar, staged exactly as the bible's image requires: she closes her notebook, stands, thanks the instructor with complete courtesy, and walks out the door with the whole room watching, the last class she will ever take at a formal institution ending at her choosing, on her feet, unhurried. The chapter holds one beat of her in the corridor after — the ladder built toward her, left standing — and lets her walk.

Afternoon: **Brom carries three boxes** — Karis's compiled materials, the research of four books, roped and balanced, carried down from the records hall through the main quadrangle in three trips he refuses help with, because the picture matters to him even if he'd never say why: the continental Copper champion, advancement found and unanswered, spending his last hours at the academy as a porter for his friend's life's work. Ephram walks the last trip beside him, carrying nothing, which is its own statement. At the gate: Withrow, who has timed her afternoon to be there without appearing to have timed anything, says goodbye in the flattest registry register — "Your files are in order. All five." — five, counted deliberately, the sixth man's name managed even now. Rooke's goodbye to Brom is four words: "Doors open both ways." The watchers across the road log the departure in plain sight, unhurried, because their paperwork was always going to outlast everyone's.

*Close on:* the coach road down the bluff at dusk, Halcenvane above the river behind them — the first institution that ever wanted them, resigned from in good standing, undefeated on paper to the last — and the log: "Not expelled. The machine had a verb ready and we didn't let it conjugate. We left. Five files closed complete. Tomorrow, the sixth."

---

**Chapter 22 — Not At His Desk**
~4,600 words

Seln's exit and the machine's answer — the two-track chapter the series uses for Compact chapters, run one last time in this configuration, and the bible's fourth image staged in its exact grammar: **Seln simply isn't at his desk the next morning.** The chapter opens in the assessment office, deliberately not in Seln's POV: the morning clerk finds the desk squared, the keys aligned on the blotter, every working file completed and cross-indexed to a standard the office will use as a template for years, and no teaching assistant. No note. No gap in the records except the man. The office does what offices do — waits a day, asks around, files a personnel query — and the query lands in the monitoring apparatus like a dropped instrument: fifteen years of product from a fully-vetted officer, review-flagged overnight, and somewhere above the file a very quiet, very expensive question beginning to be asked about what else the officer kept. The chapter gives the reader one window, brief, final for this book, Seln on the east road a day ahead of the others by tradecraft habit: the last null report, drafted in his head from pure fifteen-year reflex — *subject's activity: routine* — and, for the first time since Book 4, not filed anywhere, because there is no desk, no chain, no cover. Just a man with a case on his shoulder, walking toward the first honest employment of his adult life. The window notes that he does not look back, and that the not-looking is unpracticed.

The second track: the machine's paperwork, faceless by design. The proceedings closed as moot; the re-evaluation order unexecuted against a person no longer within any enrollment; the asset-restriction inventory filed *pending jurisdiction* — the registry's grammar for a hand closing on air. Vastin's beat, the arc's last note this book: the closure summary crosses his desk as a courtesy copy, with the resignation filings appended, and the window — three sentences, no more — watches him read the five names and the personnel query on the sixth, understand the whole shape at once, as he was always going to, and return the summary to its folder unannotated. The man who wrote *continue observation* into the file three books ago writes nothing into it now. That is the entire beat, and the book intends it to land like a door.

*Close on:* the east road, the second evening, the six of them rejoined at the crossroads camp Seln chose — the found family complete, employed by no one, enrolled in nothing — and the chapter's last image: the case and the three boxes stacked together under the same canvas, the two archives of the whole story riding in one cart. The log: "Six of us. No desks. He was at the crossroads before us with a fire already built, which I understand is the closest thing to a confession of relief we will ever get."

