v3-runs/book-07/packets/ch01-editor-prompt.md:1009:**Lowmarch:** The edge territories' one real town — a river-ford settlement three days east of the Line, built where the last good road forks into the void roads. It has a contract board, a ferry, two inns, a smith, no wall, and a population that turns over every season. The **board** is the book's institutional machine, and the series treats it the way it treated the Ironyard and the Crown ladder: rules, prices, failure modes. Contracts are posted by holds, carters, and brokers; independents take them; completion is witnessed and entered in the board-keeper's ledger; the ledger is the only reputation that exists out here, and it is *earned by outcome, not tier* — the Book 1 circuit's honesty at the scale of a country. **Pike** keeps the board: 60s, one arm, retired Copper Force Path who worked the void roads for thirty years and now prices the people who do. Pike is not Vell and not Dace; he is the third ledger-keeper of the series and the first who has no institution above him at all. His ledger entries are the book's third-party verdicts (charter §3.5).
v3-runs/book-07/packets/ch01-editor-prompt.md:1252:The circle, that night, seven for the first time — Oryn included, by Cael's decision and nobody's objection: the fact, stated plainly; the four who saw, saying what they saw; Karis's five findings on the table; Seln's professional read of what it means that the Compact measured these circles and filed them ("They know something turns Paths off. They *don't* know something doesn't turn off. Yet."); Oryn's clinical one ("My hands stop at that line. Yours don't. I had my hands in you for twenty minutes and I can tell you what I didn't find, which is a Path. That's all I've got. I'm not going to guess past it."). Nobody theorizes a maker. Nobody says *why*. The chapter's discipline is that seven intelligent people look at the largest fact of the book and agree, honestly, that they cannot see its edges — and decide, honestly, what to do anyway: the Fallow Ring is one site; Pike said a handful; the carter's map has three circles; the contract that sent them was written inside. "We find the others," Karis says. "All of them. And we find out whether they're the same." The book's second half, decided.
v3-runs/book-07/packets/ch01-editor-prompt.md:1351:The fourth site — sighted, paced, not entered — and the road chapter that earns the ending. Karis's finding, delivered at the marsh with the formality she reserves for findings she is afraid of: the Ring, the Stair, and the Hall are on a line. Not near one. *On* one — three centers, a straightedge, and the kit's ledger's bearing running through all three to the stride. And the line does not stop at the Hall; it runs on, east and south, and the carter's map's fourth circle sits on it, two days away. "Three points make a line," she says. "Four make an argument." The unit goes to look.
v3-runs/book-07/packets/ch01-editor-prompt.md:1364:The last chapter — the ledger, the map, and the door into Book 8, in the series' Chapter 24 register: small leavings and stayings, each heavier than its size. Lowmarch in first snow: the ferry's last run before the ice; Pike's ledger, and the entry he writes for the season, read aloud because the procedure requires it and the book has earned the third ledger-keeper's verdict: *Crew of seven. Ford cleared, traverse mapped, Ring entered, Stair entered and instruments recovered, Hall entered. No losses. Returned every time.* He does not enter a line number. He has stopped numbering them, and says so: "Teague says you need a column I don't have. I've kept this ledger thirty years. First time it's been wrong about a crew by being too small." Teague's crew winters at Lowmarch too, and the book gives the rival clock its last beat of the year: Teague, at the board, to Cael, without preamble — "Spring. Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes." Not an offer. A term. Cael: "Noted."
v3-runs/book-07/packets/ch01-editor-prompt.md:1817:**The proceedings:** convened at the registry seat at Ostrand (established geography — no new venue), on a term-length schedule. **Procurator Jent** appears for the Compact: 50s, colorless, exact — a career instrument who has argued four hundred enrollment matters and won the ones he was sent to win; the book keeps him deliberately small, competent, and unhateable, because hating Jent would let the system off the hook. The presiding registrar is identified in the record by seal alone — unnamed by design (the old-yard-owner convention, deployed structurally: the face the proceedings show is nobody's). **Ilsev** holds the evaluation seat — empaneled precisely because her scruples are unimpeachable, which the faceless layer above her understands as camouflage. **Havel** serves on records rotation. **Vastin** is NOT called — the Compact's best evaluator, uncalled, because his findings are already on record and already inconvenient; his petition for standing is denied in Chapter 5.
v3-runs/book-07/packets/ch01-editor-prompt.md:1849:The trap anatomized — the chapter that does for the classification challenge what Book 3's early chapters did for registry law and Book 5's did for tournament scoring: teaches the reader the machine before the machine moves. Karis, Bracken, and Withrow's counsel take the instrument apart clause by clause across a long night in the records hall, and the chapter walks the findings in order of arrival. It does not allege fraud — Greyvane closed that. It does not contest the assay clause — Vastin's own advisory closed that, and the challenge *cites his advisory approvingly*, which Karis flags as the first genuinely frightening thing in the document: they've stopped arguing with the text. It asserts an evaluation-integrity question and petitions for compelled re-evaluation under standard Arbiter protocol. Bracken supplies the procedure for evaluations that return no valid classification, reading from the registry code in his flattest voice: nullification, suspension, asset-restriction. Withrow supplies what asset-restriction means, from twenty years inside: "Everything with his name on it stops. Records, sponsorship, endorsements, travel. It's expulsion that follows you."
v3-runs/book-07/packets/ch01-editor-prompt.md:1851:Karis's structural finding is the chapter's spine, delivered with the formality she reserves for findings she doesn't like: the challenge is unpassable *by design without a designer* — the Arbiter protocol simply has no bin for [SHATTERED], and the procedure for no-bin is codified, so the outcome is determined before the first session sits. "It's not a question. It's a corridor. Every door in it is real law, and every door opens one way." And her second finding, said almost in passing, which Chapter 16 will detonate: the assay clause worked for three books because it bypasses Arbiter evaluation entirely — and this instrument's one non-negotiable demand is that Cael stand in front of an Arbiter. "They're not trying to beat the provision. They're trying to move you back inside the instrument. I don't know why that distinction feels important. Noting it."
v3-runs/book-07/packets/ch01-editor-prompt.md:1884:The Vastin chapter — first beat of the book's instrument arc, staged in the limited third-person window the series reserves for him. The proceedings' evaluation architecture crosses his desk the way everything about this file has crossed his desk for three books — except this time as a courtesy copy, not a query. The window watches him read the challenge with forty years of craft and reach the same finding Karis reached in Chapter 2, faster: the corridor, the one-way doors, the re-evaluation that is not an evaluation. And the window watches what he does about it, because doing is his language: he petitions for standing to submit an evaluator's finding to the proceedings — the continent's senior-most evaluator, the only Compact official who has personally assessed the enrollee, offering the proceedings his findings under his own name.
v3-runs/book-07/packets/ch01-editor-prompt.md:1886:The denial arrives in four days, in flawless registry prose: *the proceedings' evaluation seat is fully constituted; supplementary findings are not contemplated by the schedule.* The window holds his stillness at the sentence. Not contemplated by the schedule. He has written a version of that sentence himself, a hundred times, in a career of declining unhelpful input — and he recognizes, with the specific vertigo of a craftsman meeting his own technique from the receiving end, what it means: the proceedings do not want the best evaluation available. The proceedings want the evaluation they were built to produce. The window's closing movement: he begins writing the finding anyway — unsolicited, uncommissioned, addressed to no one — because an evaluator evaluates, and files it in his own records under a designation he has never used before: *pending a proceeding worth sending it to.*
v3-runs/book-07/packets/ch01-editor-prompt.md:2020:The analysis — Karis's chapter, the researcher the series built across four books aimed at the primary source of her life, and the chapter where every long-carried thread earns its keep. Structured as the annex's second night: the cache mapped, cross-referenced against four books of accumulated record, findings entered in Karis's formal register one by one, each landing on a thread the reader has carried for years.
