v3-runs/book-07/reports/ch01-author.json:125:    "The card's close-on names Thornwater as the log's location and the packet's outcome names Pike's ledger line 'arriving by the carter's return' as the third-party verdict; to honor both, the unit stays four days at Thornwater and the carter's second load brings Pike's copied line, so the closing log is written at Thornwater with the line in hand. Consequence: the chapter's close falls on the forty-sixth day past the Line rather than the forty-second; the log still says 'Six weeks.'",
v3-runs/book-07/reports/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.pre-sol-r1.md:49:**Lowmarch:** The edge territories' one real town — a river-ford settlement three days east of the Line, built where the last good road forks into the void roads. It has a contract board, a ferry, two inns, a smith, no wall, and a population that turns over every season. The **board** is the book's institutional machine, and the series treats it the way it treated the Ironyard and the Crown ladder: rules, prices, failure modes. Contracts are posted by holds, carters, and brokers; independents take them; completion is witnessed and entered in the board-keeper's ledger; the ledger is the only reputation that exists out here, and it is *earned by outcome, not tier* — the Book 1 circuit's honesty at the scale of a country. **Pike** keeps the board: 60s, one arm, retired Copper Force Path who worked the void roads for thirty years and now prices the people who do. Pike is not Vell and not Dace; he is the third ledger-keeper of the series and the first who has no institution above him at all. His ledger entries are the book's third-party verdicts (charter §3.5).
v3-runs/book-07/reports/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.pre-sol-r1.md:286:The circle, that night, seven for the first time — Oryn included, by Cael's decision and nobody's objection: the fact, stated plainly; the four who saw, saying what they saw; Karis's five findings on the table; Seln's professional read of what it means that the Compact measured these circles and filed them ("They know something turns Paths off. They *don't* know something doesn't turn off. Yet."); Oryn's clinical one ("Whatever's in the ring doesn't have a hold on you because there's nothing in you for it to hold. I felt that. I didn't have a word for it either."). Nobody theorizes a maker. Nobody says *why*. The chapter's discipline is that seven intelligent people look at the largest fact of the book and agree, honestly, that they cannot see its edges — and decide, honestly, what to do anyway: the Fallow Ring is one site; Pike said a handful; the carter's map has three circles; the contract that sent them was written inside. "We find the others," Karis says. "All of them. And we find out whether they're the same." The book's second half, decided.
v3-runs/book-07/reports/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.pre-sol-r1.md:385:The fourth site — sighted, paced, not entered — and the road chapter that earns the ending. Karis's finding, delivered at the marsh with the formality she reserves for findings she is afraid of: the Ring, the Stair, and the Hall are on a line. Not near one. *On* one — three centers, a straightedge, and the kit's ledger's bearing running through all three to the stride. And the line does not stop at the Hall; it runs on, east and south, and the carter's map's fourth circle sits on it, two days away. "Three points make a line," she says. "Four make an argument." The unit goes to look.
v3-runs/book-07/reports/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.pre-sol-r1.md:398:The last chapter — the ledger, the map, and the door into Book 8, in the series' Chapter 24 register: small leavings and stayings, each heavier than its size. Lowmarch in first snow: the ferry's last run before the ice; Pike's ledger, and the entry he writes for the season, read aloud because the procedure requires it and the book has earned the third ledger-keeper's verdict: *Crew of seven. Ford cleared, traverse mapped, Ring entered, Stair entered and instruments recovered, Hall entered. No losses. Returned every time.* He does not enter a line number. He has stopped numbering them, and says so: "Ghent says you need a column I don't have. I've kept this ledger thirty years. First time it's been wrong about a crew by being too small." Ghent's crew winters at Lowmarch too, and the book gives the rival clock its last beat of the year: Ghent, at the board, to Cael, without preamble — "Spring. Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes." Not an offer. A term. Cael: "Noted."
v3-runs/book-07/reports/arch-editor-sol.md:121:## Taste concerns (NEEDS_HUMAN_JUDGMENT — not defects)
v3-runs/book-07/reports/arch-editor-sol.md:124:- Pike is structurally well differentiated from Vell and Dace, but he is still the third ledger-keeper delivering third-party verdicts in closely related “pricing” language. An audio rehearsal should decide whether performance alone supplies enough separation.
v3-runs/book-07/reports/arch-editor-sol.md:127:- The board ceasing to number the crew is satisfying but closely rhymes with prior “unscorable / no column” verdicts. Human judgment should decide whether this is deliberate series motif or one repetition too many.
v3-runs/book-07/reports/arch-editor-sol.lastmsg.md:5:Verdict: **STRUCTURAL_HOLD**. The report contains 12 verified findings across all A–J dimensions. No other files were modified.

