six-count references
15:*The last of the westbound couriers turned back here: a woman on a mule with a satchel of post, who took the two letters I have carried since the first camp and said this was as far as post went. Past the ford it is carters or nothing. She looked at the six of us and did not ask what we were. I waited for the question the whole time she was tightening her girth. It did not come. I am writing down that it did not come.*
31:*Fourteen postings. Six escorts. Three clearances — two fence-lines, one ford. Two recoveries — a cart in a wash, a load off a bank. Three hold contracts, priced high, with a word on each in the board-keeper's hand: HOLD, meaning a crew is on it. Every slip prices the job. Not one slip prices the crew. There is no line on any of them for what a crew has to be — no "Iron or above," no "certified," nothing — and I looked for it the way you look for a stair in the dark, with a foot, because every board I have ever read had it.*
33:*Sorted by what each would cost the six of us: the escorts cost time. The fence-lines cost Brom's back. The wash costs rope and Lira. The ford clearance I can't price, because I don't know what is in the ford.*
37:*He opened a heading for us. Six names down the margin of a fresh page, in his hand, spelled the way we said them. Five columns ruled beside the names: Job. Hirer. Witness. Outcome. Price. I read it upside down, because I read everything upside down, and then read it again to be sure I had not missed the column every ledger I have ever been entered in has had, the one that says what you are. I had not missed it. The page does not have it.*
41:*First contract. Ferry escort: a day on the ferry, six crossings, with a broker's bales and a broker who wanted six people visible on his goods. Nothing happened. Two marks for standing where we could be seen, which is a job I have done before, in a gallery, at a higher price and for worse people. Pike's line, read upside down: escort, ferry, six crossings, no loss, two marks. The broker made his mark under "witness." The line does not say who we are. It says what we did.*
45:*Second contract. Fence-line clearance at a steading a half-day north — a mile of posts choked in thorn, too few people to cut it before the stock came down. Three days, bill-hooks and gloves. Brom cut for six hours without stopping. Karis lit nothing; she was asked not to, and did not ask why, and wrote the not-asking in her notebook, underlined. Four marks and a sack of flour, and the steading's woman said "Cheap," to nobody, in the tone of a woman who intends to hire us again.*
59:Cael had him catalogued before he sat. Fifty, or a road's version of fifty. A face weather had done most of the work on. Hands that had learned exactly two things, a rein and a wheel, and had stopped taking on new ones. A way of standing that kept his mule between himself and anything he had not priced yet, which at present was the six of them. The mule looked at Brom, because animals looked at Brom.
61:"Pike says you're six," the carter said, "and cheap."
63:"We're six," said Lira.
133:Brom's hand had not moved on the mule's cheek-strap. Cael had watched Brom answer anything with teeth for six years, and the answer had always been the same: to become the thing teeth broke on. He was not becoming it. He was walking at a mule's shoulder in the near dark with his shoulders down and his breathing slow, exactly as large as his body made him and not one degree larger, and once, when the buckle jingled, he said "Easy" to the mule in a voice that carried nowhere at all.
157:Item. It kept its distance. Forty meters at the half-mile, forty at the six hundredth pace, forty when Lira slowed for a rut and forty when she found her pace again. It did not close. It did not fall back. It was not stalking; stalking closed. It was keeping station.
165:Item — and this was the one he wrote down first that night, because it was the only one that was about them and not about it. It reacted to nothing they did. It had stopped when Lira stopped and walked when she walked and turned its head to a buckle, and in a mile of road it had done nothing in answer to six people, because in a mile of road six people had done nothing to answer.
167:That was the whole tactic. He saw it whole at about the thousandth pace, walking, with his hands empty and his eyes on a grey that would not be looked at: the thing was waiting for something, and the something was the one thing the carters did not do, and the reason the carter had hired six strangers who were paper was not their steel and not their backs. It was that paper knew how to be quiet. He had been quiet in front of a hearing. He had been quiet in front of a delegation. He had held nine things to nothing in front of every instrument the continent owned, and the instruments had marked him down for it, and here was a valley where it was the only competence that priced.
187:"Trouble?" the woman said. She said it to the carter. She had looked at the six of them for exactly as long as it took to count them.
235:*Forty-sixth day. Thornwater. First roof since the Line.*
237:*Six weeks. Four contracts. Nobody has asked what I am. Nobody has asked what any of us are — the gate-woman asked if we were paper, and I said yes, and that was the end of her interest. The board asks what we did. Pike writes it down. The line about us is one line long and every word in it is true, and that is the whole system, and I have been looking for its other half for four days and there isn't one.*
243:*Lira could have cleared it and said so once. Brom held a mule. Karis looked and did not light. Seln said five sentences in a mile and none of them was a reason. Six people held everything they own for a mile of road in the dark, and nobody had to be told twice, and that is the finding of the six weeks: we are a unit in a country that has never seen one.*

