books/book-01-the-shattered/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:408:- [ ] Coss: agent status confirmed, but his Compact rank is not clarified — OPEN
books/book-02-iron-circuit/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:4:**Cael's age: 15–16 across this book**
books/book-02-iron-circuit/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:36:- The Circuit Master coordinates scheduling and manages disputes. Not a practitioner.
books/book-02-iron-circuit/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:111:The chapter is mostly interiority, grounded in a training session with Lira. She's pushing him in a specific direction today: she has identified that his evasion framework (Wind-adjacent deployment) has a seam — a gap in the coverage angle that she can exploit with her own Wind techniques. She's not exposing it to be difficult. She wants him to find it and close it, because she knows that anyone who's fought enough Wind practitioners will find it eventually. Their training is specific and useful and occasionally funny in the way that training becomes funny when two people know each other well enough to be honest about what's not working.
books/book-02-iron-circuit/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:170:"You're continuing to reside in an Unranked District?" "Yes." "You're not engaged in guild-affiliated practice?" "I don't have guild affiliation." "You're aware that your classification status requires you to maintain current contact with—" "I'm aware of my obligations."
books/book-02-iron-circuit/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:183:Brom is 16. He came to Ardenmere two months ago from his family's estate in Velmere, having walked out before they could formally stage the response to his Kindling result. Iron Skin Path, Copper-tier. His family expected Gold — the kind of expectation that produces either a practitioner who lives up to it or one who spends a decade being measured against something they weren't. Brom chose a third option: left before the measuring started. He's been doing circuit work in two other cities. He came to Ardenmere because its circuit is known in the practitioner network for accepting the most unusual competitors.
books/book-02-iron-circuit/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:234:**First exchange:** Cael tests his transition-window hypothesis. He tries to force rapid alternation between Brom's defensive and offensive activation modes by varying impact speed and angle. Brom is patient and absorbs everything — every hit Cael lands is met with redistributed force, the Iron Skin's amplification returning energy at an unexpected angle. No window is visible. Brom wins the exchange without taking damage.
books/book-02-iron-circuit/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:236:**Second exchange:** Cael shifts strategy. He tries speed over impact, looking for a moment where Brom's read hasn't fully engaged. Brom adapts. His pressure-read operates at a faster response rate than Cael's observation suggested — he's modified the read's sensitivity to compensate for fast approaches, probably because he's fought opponents who tried this before. Cael takes a real hit. His legs are compromised — not broken, but unstable. He knows he has two exchanges at best before his mobility degrades enough to end the fight.
books/book-02-iron-circuit/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:330:Brom, to Lira, unprompted: "The Wind technique you're using in the circuit isn't what the standard manuals describe." Lira, flatly: "I improved it." Brom: "I know. You should be competing at Iron-equivalent." She stares at him. He means it. She turns to Cael: "I like him." Cael: "I thought you might."
books/book-02-iron-circuit/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:347:Cael doesn't hide or confront. He continues training. He writes in his log: "Different monitoring agent. Patient. No approach. Purely observational. They want to know what I do, not whether I'm in compliance." He notes this is a different mandate than Havel's, which means different instructions from a different level of the Compact's chain.
books/book-02-iron-circuit/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:362:Cael pages through the oldest section carefully. He finds the entry. A practitioner — name partially damaged by age — described as fighting with abilities that don't map to any guild classification the Ledger-keeper recognized. The bout record is otherwise standard. The Ledger-keeper's marginal note reads: "Assessed per pre-registry terminology as [UNBOUND]. Abilities not matching any guild standard. Competed without incident."
books/book-02-iron-circuit/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:451:**First exchange:** Reydan opens without preamble. His burst compression is everything the accounts described: tight, fast, minimal wind-up. The containment signature is present — Cael reads it, Lira's evasion drilling takes over, and he avoids the first full burst entirely. He doesn't land anything. Reydan wins the exchange in neutral — no damage on either side. He's reading Cael's evasion pattern.
books/book-02-iron-circuit/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:478:What she offers, stated without preamble: "The Greyvane Academy has a provision called the demonstration-provision track. It was written for unkindled prospects under observation. You're demonstrably beyond unkindled. But the provision's language applies to any practitioner without a standard Arbiter-issued classification, which you qualify for — and we've been looking for the right candidate for some time." Cael: "The Compact monitors me." Quenna: "We're aware. We have institutional legal staff." Cael: "My Path classification is—" Quenna: "Our assessment staff will evaluate you monthly by demonstration. That's the provision's requirement. What you did tonight qualifies as demonstrably observable practice." She pauses. "We're not asking what your Path is. We're asking whether you can demonstrate it consistently."
books/book-02-iron-circuit/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:518:Note: force absorption component. Damage redirect, contact range.
books/book-02-iron-circuit/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:523:The leavings: Vell gives him a copy of his circuit record — the official ledger pages, in her handwriting. Every bout, every rating, every notable exchange. "The records know you existed here. No one gets to say otherwise." He folds the pages and puts them with Hesk's notebook in his pack.
books/book-02-iron-circuit/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:539:- [ ] **No SECRET disclosed before reveal book:** The integration mechanism (absorbing witnessed abilities) is approaching its Book 3 reveal — Book 2 establishes that fragments arrive from observation without naming the mechanism explicitly. "I borrowed the shape of how it moves" language from Book 1 continues; the word "integration" is used in Cael's private log but never explained to other characters as a system.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:4:**Cael's age: seventeen at open (verified against Book 5's close — the on-page birthday was B5 Chapter 14, at Norhold); turns EIGHTEEN on-page in Chapter 14 — the series' third staged birthday, deliberately the smallest yet and deliberately the last one staged inside an institution's walls. No silent drift: every age reference before Ch14 reads seventeen, every reference after reads eighteen**
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:5:**Time skip: ~two months between the road home from Norhold and Chapter 1 — the winter term opening after the tournament year, staged explicitly in Chapter 1's opening movement, log-voice, dated and inventoried per the B4/B5 precedent**
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:20:The arc statement is staged honestly, in the log, at both poles: the first sentence is written — confident, earned across three books of hearings and clauses and stamps, and wrong at a depth Cael cannot yet see — in Chapter 2. The second is written at a campfire beyond the Registry Line in Chapter 24. The whole book is the distance between the two entries, and the book's cruelest discipline is that the first sentence keeps *winning its arguments* the entire way down. Cael does not lose within the system. He learns that winning within it was never the variable being measured.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:28:- [ ] **Book 6 delivery (this book — the challenge):** The Compact resolves the threat-classification question Book 5's Ch24 convening opened by filing a formal classification challenge (served Chapter 1, sustained to full proceedings Chapter 8, ruled Chapter 18). The challenge, exactly per the bible: it would remove Cael's demonstration-provision enrollment and compel him to submit to a Compact re-evaluation — which he would fail, because the Compact cannot evaluate [SHATTERED] architecture, and their procedure for failed evaluations is expulsion and asset-restriction. The trap's anatomy is anatomized on the page (Chapter 2) and the B4 checkpoint's cross-check is honored explicitly: the falsification machinery lives in *Arbiter evaluation*; the assay clause bypasses Arbiter records entirely, which is precisely why it could hold Cael — and the challenge's entire design is to herd him out of the one legal stratum the machinery cannot reach and back into the stratum it owns. Karis says the first half of this in Chapter 2 without knowing why it's true. Chapter 16 pays the sentence off in full.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:29:- [ ] **Book 6 delivery (this book — THE REVELATION; SERIES SECRET #2 DISCHARGED ON SCHEDULE):** Seln provides his intelligence cache — the files he collected during his years as a Compact operative, carried through nineteen postings in the locked case Book 4 established and Book 5 kept visible — opened on the page in Chapter 15, analyzed in Chapter 16. The files confirm: **the Guilds Compact has been falsifying Path classifications for two hundred years.** Practitioners who develop politically inconvenient abilities are reclassified into lesser Paths at their Kindling evaluation, via a modification built into the Arbiter evaluation protocol itself — a modification made so long ago that no current Compact official knows it's there. REACH discipline is absolute: the revelation is known at book's end to exactly seven people — the inner circle (Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis), Seln, Vastin (Chapter 17), and no one else. It is NOT published, NOT filed, NOT used in the proceedings, and the book argues why on the page (Chapter 19): evidence of the Arbiter protocol's corruption cannot be verified by any process that runs on Arbiters, and filing it burns Seln for nothing. The truth is carried out the door, not detonated. Its public exposure belongs to later books.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:30:- [ ] **Book 6 delivery (the Level 4 payoff — the flag finally MEANS something):** The Suppression-Advisory Watch Level 4 flag — planted B2 Ch7/Ch15, carried by Havel's private note since Ardenmere and Ilsev's referrals since the Greyvane hearing, returned "no originating authority of record" (B4) and then "closed — no further inquiry authorized" (B5) — is connected, explicitly and on the page (Chapter 16), to the falsification machinery. The staging: the cache's protocol-era maintenance fragment documents the evaluation sub-layer's "advisory corrections" function — the falsification mechanism itself; and the sub-layer's *protection behavior* is what Ilsev's paper trail has been photographing for three books. When the Arbiter meets an architecture it cannot reclassify into a lesser Path — because [SHATTERED] has no lesser bin — the corrections function cannot fire, and what fires instead is the suppression-advisory flag: the machinery's contingency for the case it cannot falsify. The returns Ilsev received are the sub-layer defending itself — auto-closing inquiries no living official authorized because no living official runs that layer. Karis lays Ilsev's on-record testimony (Chapter 18 enters the returns' exact text into public record; Karis obtains the transcript) beside the cache's fragment and says the payoff line: "She's been interrogating the mechanism for three books and filing its answers. She just never knew what was answering." Ilsev and Havel do NOT learn the truth — their notes mean something to the READER and to the circle; the carriers themselves are left intact, unknowing, and available (see below). **One deliberate residue kept open for Book 8:** the sub-layer's base stratum is demonstrably OLDER than the two-hundred-year modification written into it — the cache documents the modification, not the layer; who built the layer, and when, is beyond the cache's reach and beyond this book's. Stated on the page, once, and locked.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:31:- [ ] **Book 6 delivery (Vastin's moment):** Chapter 17, when the companions bring him the intelligence cache — the trust built in small increments across Books 4–5 (the advisory that let Cael onto the roster, the attendance, the warning) spent all at once. He is silent for a long time. Then the bible's exact line, verbatim: ***"I knew the system was imperfect. I didn't know it was designed to be."*** He does NOT switch sides in this book — he takes no companion's oath, joins no walkout, shares no files of his own, and remains inside the Compact at book's end. What he stops doing is being its instrument: he refuses to certify the re-evaluation protocol's applicability (the signature the trap wanted for legitimacy), recuses himself from all [SHATTERED]-adjacent evaluation permanently, and — the line he actually crosses — files nothing about the cache. An Archmarshal in possession of evidence of the falsification, reporting nothing: the keeper pattern, rhymed deliberately with Seln's Book 4 arc, at forty years' altitude. The escalation is staged in beats (Ch5 denied standing, Ch17 the cache, Ch18 the refused certification, Ch22 the non-action) so it reads as one man's forty-year covenant with his instruments ending, not a heel-face turn. His eventual departure from the Compact and what he takes from the classified history section when he goes (Book 8) are NOT staged here — he is still inside when the book closes. Do not resolve.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:32:- [ ] **Book 6 delivery (the walk-out):** Chapters 21–22, with the bible's four images staged verbatim: the companions leave Halcenvane together — not expelled, they **resign** (timed before the compelled re-evaluation date, converting the machine's expulsion into their refusal); **Karis forfeits her ranking** (the procedural act on the page — Iron-tier Rank 3 and her research standing, signed away at Bracken's counter); **Lira walks out of the last class she'll ever take at a formal institution** (mid-session, unhurried, the door closing on the Silver trajectory Book 5 built for exactly this cost); **Brom carries three boxes of Karis's compiled materials** (the Book 5 pending-advancement-review thread paid off here — see below — the review is what he's walking away from, and the boxes are what he chooses to carry instead); **Seln simply isn't at his desk the next morning** (Chapter 22, staged in his register: no scene of decision, no goodbye, keys and files in perfect order, the man and the case gone).
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:33:- [ ] **Book 6 delivery (the ending / Arc 3 handoff):** Chapter 24 — the companions in the edge territories for the first time, beyond the Registry Line, with no institutional backing, no ranking protection, and a Compact classification file that now reads, verbatim: ***active non-compliance, observation priority escalated.*** Cael's fragment log contains **NINE integrated abilities** by book's close. He still doesn't have a tier. The edge territories are staged as the literal handoff into Arc 3 ("The World," Books 7–9) — the door Book 7 walks through. The Quieting is NOT introduced, named, hinted, or foreshadowed — it is Book 7's first encounter and nothing in this book brushes it. Oryn remains reserved and unmet; NO Tide Path practitioner appears on-page anywhere in this book (drafting constraint carried forward from Book 5, for the same reason).
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:34:- [ ] **Fragment deliveries (two, reaching nine):** The bible's close requires nine integrated abilities; Book 5 closed at seven confirmed. This book stages exactly two acquisitions. **Eighth — Anchor-adjacent (Chapter 13):** acquired mid-combat from the unnamed containment specialist of the off-channel interception team on the Ostrand road — adversarial, earnest, ultimate stakes, the purest classical conditions since Reydan, and the first fragment in the log whose source has no name (the system finally sent someone namelessly, and the log marks the wrongness). First Silver-equivalent notice. **Ninth — Blade-adjacent (Chapter 20):** acquired in the farewell bout Ephram demands before the walkout — full-earnest, sanctioned, "show me straight" — the stakes rule satisfied on the Daeva/Reydan precedent (earnest engagement, no mechanism disclosure; Ephram does NOT learn the SECRET and does not consent to what he cannot know about, and the log carries that cost honestly, in writing, in advance, per the B4 Ch9 covenant). Both notices shown in full on the page in the established format.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:35:- [ ] **Book 6 delivery (Seln comes inside — the asymmetry-debt PAID):** Chapter 15. When Seln opens the cache, Cael answers in kind: the mechanism — the Book 3 SECRET — disclosed to Seln in full, the first expansion of the circle since Karis, making it six. And the debt Book 4 Ch14 filed as "no current payment mechanism" is paid on the page: Cael tells him about Shadow-adjacent — acquired during the records-office operation Seln let him watch — and about the seal: that through the tournament, through the banking's death, through a Gold-tier match he nearly lost, Shadow-adjacent stayed sealed because exposing it would have burned its source. Seln's response is one sentence and is the closest thing to being moved the series will ever show him (drafting note: keep it under ten words). The seal STAYS on publicly after this book — the logic updates (a defector's tradecraft in Cael's hands would confirm the surveillance-era acquisition) but the discipline holds.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:38:- [ ] **Book 8 plant (advanced one notch, absolutely not resolved):** [UNBOUND]. The word appears in exactly ONE chapter this book (Chapter 16, the analysis) and nowhere else: Karis lays her copied Greyvane passage and the Vell-archive marginal note beside the cache's oldest document and dates the strata — the falsification modification, the standardization directive, and the renaming of [UNBOUND] are the *same era, same authors*: the scheme's founders retired the old word when they buried what it described. That is the whole advance. What [UNBOUND] means, what it was, why it "nearly destroyed" anything, who the renamers feared — none of it is in the cache (Seln's files are operational, two-hundred-year scope; the older mystery is explicitly beyond their reach, and the chapter says so). The book states the distinctness rule on the page, in Karis's voice: the falsification is two hundred years old; the layer it's written into is older; the word is older still. "We've explained the middle of the story. Not the beginning." LOCKED until Book 8.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:39:- [ ] **LOCKED — must stay open (the Tide-adjacent anomaly):** Book 2, session nine. This book resolves the series' second SECRET, which makes the discipline here MORE important, not less: the Tide anomaly is NOT the falsification, is NOT explained by the falsification, and must not be allowed to blur toward it in drafting. The falsification operates at Kindling evaluation on *other people's* classifications; the anomaly is an unexplained event inside Cael's own architecture — different mystery, different era, different book (its resolution is reserved far past Arc 2). Handling: log-inventory references ONLY — Chapter 14 and Chapter 24, the standing three words ("Still open. Still real. Patience."). In Chapter 16, when the cache is fully mapped, ONE sentence of explicit non-connection is permitted and required: Cael checks the anomaly against everything the cache explains and writes "Not this either. Still open." Fragment-count math everywhere treats it as an anomaly entry, never counted. NO Tide Path practitioner on-page anywhere in this book.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:40:- [ ] **LOCKED — untouched threads:** The patient Iron Skin watcher (B2 Ch15), the Book 1 market stranger, Hesk's full history (letter beats only — Ch7's letter is a letter beat and stays at that altitude), Vell's offered archive session (unclaimed; at most a letter reference), Coss's exact grade (Coss himself offstage; at most one passing mention). Reydan's "find me later — I want the answer" is touched in exactly one log line (Ch24, beside Daeva's) and not advanced. Vastin's null-report-pattern detection: still NOT taken — he never learns how long Seln's product was empty, in this book or from this book.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:51:- *The trap, anatomized:* the re-evaluation is unpassable by design — not rigged, which would require someone to rig it, but *structurally incapable*: the Arbiter protocol has no category for [SHATTERED] architecture (the series' founding fact), and the procedure for an evaluation that returns no valid classification is codified: enrollment nullification, practitioner-status suspension, and **asset-restriction** — the registry's mechanism for freezing an unclassified person's standing, records, sponsorships, travel endorsements, and institutional relationships. Expulsion, industrialized.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:55:**The proceedings:** convened at the registry seat at Ostrand (established geography — no new venue), on a term-length schedule. **Procurator Jent** appears for the Compact: 50s, colorless, exact — a career instrument who has argued four hundred enrollment matters and won the ones he was sent to win; the book keeps him deliberately small, competent, and unhateable, because hating Jent would let the system off the hook. The presiding registrar is identified in the record by seal alone — unnamed by design (the old-yard-owner convention, deployed structurally: the face the proceedings show is nobody's). **Ilsev** holds the evaluation seat — empaneled precisely because her scruples are unimpeachable, which the faceless layer above her understands as camouflage. **Havel** serves on records rotation. **Vastin** is NOT called — the Compact's best evaluator, uncalled, because his findings are already on record and already inconvenient; his petition for standing is denied in Chapter 5.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:57:**Anchor Path (new Path, defined this book — PROVISIONAL):** containment architecture — anchored force lattices, perimeter binding, suppression fields laid as fixed points rather than projected effects. Distinct from Shield Path (personal defensive declaration) in kind, not degree: Shield protects the self; Anchor cages the other. Rare, unglamorous, and disproportionately represented in Compact enforcement service, for the obvious institutional reason the book does not belabor: it is the Path the system uses to hold people. Its appearance in the off-channel team (Chapters 11, 13) is itself evidence of who sent them. Naming note: "Ward Path" was drafted and REJECTED for root-collision with the Warden rank (Coss's title) — see the naming checkpoint.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:74:The winter term after the tournament year, staged in the log-voice opening movement the way Books 4 and 5 staged their skips — dated, inventoried, done: the road home from Norhold; two months of a campus that treats the delegation like returning weather (third on the continent changes how a mid-tier academy walks); Lira's Silver-bracket preparation program opening under Rooke with institutional blessing; Brom's advancement review docketed for term's end; Karis's research standing renewed with an actual budget attached; Seln at his desk, at the table now when the table assembles, which nobody remarks on and everybody counts; the watchers outside the walls back to their patient rotation — thinner than Norhold, more senior than last year, which Cael notes without yet knowing what it means. He is seventeen, and says so on the page. Seven confirmed fragments — the Storm-adjacent entry's stability flag cleared over the winter, the integration finally settled, logged with relief. One anomaly. A match record the continent is still arguing about.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:76:The chapter's centerpiece is the arrival: a registry courier under formal escort, a sealed instrument addressed to the Chancellor and the enrollee of record jointly — protocol nobody at Halcenvane has ever seen used. Withrow reads it standing. The chapter gives the reader the first page only, in the document's own voice: *a formal challenge to enrollment basis... compelled re-evaluation under standard Arbiter protocol... given under the Compact's hand.* The convening Book 5 closed on has produced its answer, and the answer has arrived wearing law.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:91:*Key beat (the first pole, on the page):* the Power Log, end of chapter: "I can work within the system. I have the receipts: a hearing, a provision, a stamp renewed four times, a tournament their own clause put me in. The system has text, and text has held me every time the people failed. This is more text. We'll read it better than they do. We always have." He is wrong, and the book knows exactly how long it will take him to find out.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:100:Withrow's stake staged honestly: the academy is the named respondent, and defending the enrollment now means Halcenvane spends its post-tournament standing — the standing Withrow built her whole tenure toward — on the continent's most dangerous student. Her scene with Cael is the chapter's quiet center. She does not reassure him. She shows him the ledger, both columns, the way she did in Book 4 Chapter 2, and then: "I read the hearing transcript four times before I ever met you. The law was on your side then and it's on your side now. I'm choosing to keep believing that matters. Make a note of the word *choosing*." Cael makes a note of the word choosing.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:109:The stakes inventory — the chapter that makes the walkout expensive eighteen chapters early, because a resignation that costs nothing earns nothing. Built as the series' braided-track chapter, one movement per companion, each stake named on the page in its owner's voice.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:111:Lira's Silver-bracket program opens: Rooke's training floor, continental-grade drills, the trajectory she declared at Norhold ("I want to become something they haven't seen yet") now an institutional program with her name on it — the first time in her life the system has built a ladder *toward* her. The chapter gives her one bout, one win, and one unguarded sentence to Cael at the rail: "Three years ago I'd have told you I didn't need any of this. I'm better now. I can admit I want it." Brom's review paperwork arrives: the mandatory advancement review, docketed for term's end, the continental Copper title making the outcome — everyone says quietly — a formality. His family's shadow crosses the page once: a second Velmere-sealed envelope, unopened for two days, then opened alone; the chapter shows nothing of its contents and one fact about his face afterward: it is the face of a man being offered his whole ledger back. Karis's standing renewed: research budget, archive privileges, a junior assistant assigned to her — the institution finally treating her the way Ternhall never did. Ephram, cohort captain now, training beside Brom like a man who has decided something and is waiting for the right term to say it.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:122:The Vastin chapter — first beat of the book's instrument arc, staged in the limited third-person window the series reserves for him. The proceedings' evaluation architecture crosses his desk the way everything about this file has crossed his desk for three books — except this time as a courtesy copy, not a query. The window watches him read the challenge with forty years of craft and reach the same finding Karis reached in Chapter 2, faster: the corridor, the one-way doors, the re-evaluation that is not an evaluation. And the window watches what he does about it, because doing is his language: he petitions for standing to submit an evaluator's finding to the proceedings — the continent's senior-most evaluator, the only Compact official who has personally assessed the enrollee, offering the proceedings his findings under his own name.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:133:The continental echo — the challenge meets the fame the tournament built, and the book stages the collision at full scale. The proceedings are public record; the enrollee is the most-reviewed match result in tournament history; the story writes itself across the continent in a dozen wrong versions (the concealed-classification theory, the fraud theory, the suppressed-Path theory — Chapter 22 of Book 5's taxonomy, now with institutional stakes). Halcenvane's post is drowned in correspondence. Umber's adjudication office formally transmits the referred tournament file to the proceedings — the *unscorable under standard* entry now a cited exhibit, which Cael turns over with bleak appreciation: "The honest men keep filing true reports, and the machine keeps using them as evidence."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:144:The asset-restriction chapter — the book's coldest early dread, staged almost entirely in paperwork and letters. Seln, running the counter-scouting nobody formally asked him for (the Book 5 pattern, continued), identifies a texture change in the surveillance: the watchers are no longer watching Cael. They are watching everything *attached* to him. Records requests touch Halcenvane's sponsorship files. A registry query pings Lira's certification history, Brom's family standing, Karis's transfer file. And the beat the chapter is built around: Hesk's letter, arriving fast as ever, dry as ever, with one paragraph that stops the table — *"Two polite men from the registry came through Denvash asking after my trade licenses and the shop's title. Wanted to know if you had any interest recorded in either. I told them my grandson's interest in this shop is that he grew up in it, and they wrote that down. Whatever they're building, it has a shape. Mind the shape. — H."*
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:146:Seln names the shape in the inner-circle council, in the flat register of a man describing his own former trade: pre-restriction inventory. Before the registry freezes an unclassified person's standing, it maps everything that standing touches — sponsors, institutions, family assets, endorsements — so the restriction lands complete on day one. It is run only when the outcome is already assumed. "They're not preparing for a ruling," he says. "They're preparing for the day after it." The chapter lets the sentence sit on the table untouched, because there is nothing to say to it — and then gives Lira the line that turns the book's second half: "So the question was never whether we win the argument. It's what we do the day after."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:170:The council chapter — the first honest collision over leaving, staged early and *lost* by the leavers, because the walkout must be earned and Cael is not ready. Lira puts it on the table plainly (Chapter 7's question, matured): the proceedings are a corridor; the day-after is being inventoried; the Registry Line is four days east; why are we still filing responses? The debate is the chapter's spine, four voices plus one silence, all honest. Karis argues the record: every prior institution failed them until text held it accountable; abandon the proceedings and the record reads *fled* — the system's version of a confession. Brom argues the middle: prepare both roads, walk neither yet. Cael's position is the first pole at full strength, argued with three books of receipts — the hearing, the provision, the stamps, the tournament — and one sentence that the book will spend its second half dismantling: "The system has never beaten me when the text was in the room. I'm not leaving the room while the text still works."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:179:Lira's chapter — the Silver-bracket debut given full featured weight, per the series' structural law that companion arcs get real page-time even while the institutional sky falls, and per this book's particular cruelty: the chapter exists to make Chapter 21 cost. Lira's first Silver-bracket meet, Rooke's program on the road, and the choreography the arc has been building since Zerin said *Silver bracket, next cycle. Be there*: she fights up a tier against a Silver Rank 8 and loses on points in the fourth exchange, extended past anything the bracket expected, and walks off the floor incandescent — because the loss is the *right kind*, the kind with six more years of doors in it, and for the first time in her life every door is open and someone is holding them. Rooke's post-bout note, four words, Rooke-length: "Again. Faster. It's there." She sleeps with the bout sheet on the table where she can see it.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:181:The chapter braids the counterweight through in two movements. First: mid-meet, the proceedings intrude — a registry records officer serves Halcenvane's delegation with a documentary demand *at the venue*, timed for maximum visibility, and Lira watches her academy's colors get quieter in the hall in real time. Second: the session-track discovery that closes the Part's legal question for good. Karis, back at Ostrand, forces Jent on the record to the admission the whole book pivots on — asked directly what evidence would defeat the petition, the Procurator answers, courteously, precisely: "None, Advocate. The petition doesn't turn on evidence. It turns on discretion." The winning-doesn't-matter sentence, finally said out loud, in a room with a transcript.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:190:The first off-channel action — Vastin's Book 5 warning materializing on the page, staged with the series' institutional precision: nothing illegal happens, which is what makes it the book's most frightening chapter to date. Four practitioners arrive at Halcenvane's gate bearing an "escort order" for the enrollee's transfer to a registry facility for *pre-evaluation assessment* — ahead of the proceedings' schedule, under a seal Bracken cannot match to any issuing office, on paper that is genuine registry stock. Withrow meets them at the gate herself with counsel and refuses on procedure: the proceedings' schedule is the schedule; no facility transfer is contemplated; the order's issuing authority is unidentifiable. The four accept the refusal without argument — which everyone present understands was itself the probe: measuring whether the paper would work, at no cost if it didn't — and leave.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:194:*Close on:* the inner circle in the records hall, the escort order's copy on the table, and Seln looking at it longer than anyone — the chapter's last image held on him, because the reader (tracking his Chapter 9 window) can see what the companions can't yet: a man watching the machine he carries the blueprints for begin to move, and running out of reasons. The log: "Vastin said the people who want this to stop wouldn't use official channels much longer. Today the unofficial channel knocked at the gate, politely, in genuine paper. It'll knock once more somewhere without a Withrow in front of it. Whatever we're going to do, the schedule isn't ours anymore."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:201:The hinge — Seln's decision completing, staged in the register Book 4 built for him: visible entirely in behavior, confessed never. The chapter opens on his window and stays there longer than the series has ever stayed: the escort order's forensics (he traced the paper stock; he knows which supply chain it walked out of; he has *run* operations on that stock), the pre-restriction inventory, the four grades of pressure he can name that come next — and against all of it, the boy's Chapter 9 sentence, quoted in Seln's own memory with professional distaste: *the text still works.* The window walks his logic one last time — the operative's catechism, fifteen years old: insurance is only insurance while it stays sealed; the moment the case opens, the man holding it stops being a keeper and becomes a witness, and witnesses do not retire — and then watches the logic lose, without drama, the way everything about Seln happens: he takes the case down. He does not open it. He carries it, for the first time in nineteen postings, out of his quarters and across the campus, and requests five minutes of Cael's evening.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:203:The conversation is four sentences long and the book stages it complete. Seln: "You said the text still works. I need to show you whose text it is. Not here — all six of us, somewhere I choose, this week." Cael, who has spent four books reading this man and knows the case by sight the way the reader does: "You're sure." Seln: "No. That's how you'll know it's true."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:212:The interception — the second off-channel action, and the series' Chapter 13 acquisition tradition (B2, B3, B4) honored a fourth time, at the highest stakes it has ever run. The annex meeting is set; the proceedings' schedule intervenes first — a documentary session at Ostrand requiring the enrollee's presence — and the delegation coach takes the river road it has taken all term. The ambush is staged with the book's off-channel signature: no uniforms, no declarations of authority, no paper at all this time — six practitioners, road discipline, and the unhurried Anchor specialist from the gate laying her lattice across the road's cut *before* the coach enters it, fixed points blooming in a geometry Cael's Iron-adjacent read maps in real time and cannot find the exit of. The purpose is transparent and the chapter states it coldly: not assault — *custody*. A detention that will be paperwork by the time anyone asks, executed where no Withrow stands.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:214:The fight is the book's first full choreography and the found family's — five practitioners plus Seln against six, on a road, for keeps. The chapter runs it in the series' register: Lira's Silver-honed speed breaking the perimeter's timing, Brom pricing the strike team's contact points, Karis's improvised-style lattice-work jamming ignition against the anchor fields, Seln simply *absent* from every sightline that matters, and Cael — the public suite spent openly, banking long dead — inside the Anchor specialist's closing geometry for four sustained exchanges, at survival intensity, ultimate stakes, an architecture in earnest adversarial use against him at contact range. The conditions the model requires, complete. The B4 Ch9 line, drawn in advance and stood on for three books, permits exactly this. He reaches. Directed. Open-eyed. Mid-fight — and unbinds her own fixed point from inside it, stepping through a gap her Path says cannot exist. The team breaks off the moment the geometry fails — deniable operations do not fight losing engagements — and is gone with a professionalism that is its own signature. The notice arrives on the road, and the page shows it in full:
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:222:Acquisition: directed. Engagement: adversarial, combat.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:225:Two firsts, counted on the page even now, because he is who he is: the first notice to say *Silver* — the tier ladder in his log now runs Bronze, Silver, Gold, none of them his. And the first fragment whose source has no name. He reads the notice twice, sitting on the road bank while Brom rights the coach, and writes the entry underneath it that the book has been building toward: "Eight. Taken from someone the system sent namelessly, to hold me namelessly. Every other piece in this log has a person attached — Lira, Feryn, Brom, Reydan, Karis, Seln, Daeva. This one has a hand and no face. I finally own a piece of the machine itself. I don't know yet what that costs. Write it down anyway."
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:234:The rest chapter — integration cost, aftermath, and the series' third staged birthday, deliberately the smallest and deliberately the last inside an institution's walls. The destabilization is paid on-page per the B3 Ch14 constraint and managed with the B4-established discipline: recovery days banked, the circle warned, nothing evaluable scheduled — and it comes anyway, real: the Anchor-adjacent component *pulls* like nothing before it, wanting to fix points in ordinary rooms, doorways going subtly wrong at the edges of his attention until he learns its idle state; the Storm-adjacent framework, heaviest neighbor, resettles twice. Karis documents the comparative data. Eighth acquisition, second Silver-or-above in two books; her finding, delivered carefully: the architecture is accelerating into heavier sources, and the recovery arithmetic is holding — "for now" entered into the record at her insistence.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:236:The birthday is staged against the term's darkening sky, and the book lets both be true at once: Lira instigates, one evening, the six of them in the residence common room with the proceedings' documents deliberately stacked face-down on the far table; Brom acquires food with quiet competence; Ephram is invited — the first non-circle guest at a birthday in the series, the invitation itself a statement the chapter does not underline; Seln sits at the table like a man who has been sitting at tables all his life, and only the reader and Cael know the case is already down from its shelf. Hesk's package, held ready as always: a travel-grade log satchel, instrument-maker's work, oiled against weather — the gift's meaning hiding in plain sight — and the note in full: *"Eighteen. A man's tools should fit the road he's actually on, not the one they promised him. Your grandmother would say I'm being unsubtle. She'd be right. Mind the shape. — H."* Cael is eighteen, on the page, witnessed, the date written into the log in his own hand.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:245:The revelation, first movement — the locked case opened, on the page, after two books of the reader watching it stay shut. The annex: Seln's chosen ground, sightlines controlled, one lamp, the six of them. He opens the case without preamble — a keeper's economy, no speech — and the chapter stages the contents physically before intellectually, because the series trusts objects: fifteen years of an intelligence officer's private duplicates. Case files with reclassification orders clipped to Kindling records that do not match them. Field reports on practitioners — some of them names Seln was assigned to watch — whose demonstrated architecture exceeded their filed Path by margins no honest evaluation survives. Registry memoranda, copies of copies, a lineage of administrative language two centuries deep, all orbiting one recurring phrase in the oldest stratum: *corrective classification at Kindling evaluation.* And the artifact at the case's floor, in a wax-paper sleeve older than everything above it: a protocol-era maintenance directive fragment — genuine, provenance documented in Seln's own cipher — referencing the Arbiter evaluation sub-layer's *advisory corrections function*.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:249:And then the chapter's second movement, the debt paid: Cael answers the case in kind. The mechanism — the Book 3 SECRET — disclosed to Seln in full, on the page, the circle's first expansion since Karis: the integration, the fragments, the notices, the log. And Shadow-adjacent: acquired during the records-office operation Seln let him watch, sealed ever since — through the tournament, through the banking's death, through four exchanges against a Gold — because exposing it would have burned its source. Seln looks at the boy who kept his cover at Gold-tier cost, for a long moment, and the book gives him his sentence (drafting note: under ten words, no warmth performed, all of it real). The keeper and the kept, each holding the other's file for years, both files now open on the same table.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:260:**Finding one — the mechanism and the flag.** The maintenance fragment's *advisory corrections function* lives in the evaluation sub-layer; the corrections fire at Kindling, rewriting inconvenient architecture into lesser Paths before the classification ever reaches a record a human reads. And the case the function cannot handle: an architecture with no lesser bin. Karis lays the proceedings transcript beside the fragment — Ilsev's Chapter 18-bound testimony already foreshadowed in her empanelment scope, the returns' exact text from Books 4 and 5 (*no originating authority of record; designation predates file creation* — *closed; no further inquiry authorized*) — and delivers the payoff three books built: the Suppression-Advisory Watch Level 4 flag is the corrections function's contingency — what the machinery does when it meets the thing it cannot falsify. The returns are the sub-layer defending itself, auto-closing inquiries no living official authorized because no living official runs that layer. "She's been interrogating the mechanism for three books and filing its answers," Karis says, the transcript in one hand and the fragment in the other. "She just never knew what was answering." (Ilsev and Havel learn nothing; the payoff is the reader's and the circle's. The carriers stay intact.)
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:262:**Finding two — the strata, kept honest.** Dating the cache's oldest documents against her own research: the falsification modification, the standardization directive, and the renaming of the old word are the same era, same administrative hand — the scheme's founders retired [UNBOUND] when they buried what it described. One controlled appearance of the word, the copied Greyvane passage physically on the table beside the cache — and then the boundary, stated in Karis's voice as method, because keeping mysteries distinct is what she is *for*: the falsification is two hundred years old; the sub-layer it is written into is demonstrably older, and the cache documents the modification, not the layer; the word is older still, and nothing in an operative's two-century paper trail can reach it. "We've explained the middle of the story. Not the beginning. Entered as a limit, not a finding." LOCKED, both threads, on the page. **Finding three — the anomaly, checked and released:** Cael, at the table's end, runs the one check the reader would demand: session nine against everything the cache explains. Nothing touches it. The log gets one line: "Not this either. Still open." **Finding four — the retroactive chill:** Chapter 2's unexplained note, paid: the challenge was never clumsy. The machinery is herding him out of the demonstration stratum — the one legal shelf its corrections cannot reach — and back inside the instrument it owns. The corridor finally has a *why*.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:275:Vastin's moment — the chapter the arc's trust-increments have been buying since Book 4, staged with the discipline the bible's constraint demands: he does not switch sides, and the chapter never once lets him. The approach is the companions' riskiest act of the series and the book prices it on the page: bringing evidence of the falsification to a serving Archmarshal is either the end of the Compact's honest man or the end of the six of them, and they cannot know which until it is done. The mechanics are Seln's — neutral ground in Ostrand, no record, the cache's core exhibits and not the cache itself (tradecraft to the last) — and the vote to do it at all is taken on the page, six hands, unanimous, Seln's raised last and meaning most.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:277:The meeting is the chapter's whole second half, and the book slows it to the pace the series reserves for its keystone conversations. Vastin reads. He reads the way he evaluated — completely, without comment, at his own speed — the reclassification orders against their Kindling records, the memoranda lineage, the maintenance fragment. The chapter holds the silence after in real time, unhurried, the longest held silence in the series — a forty-year evaluator auditing the instrument he gave his life to, finding the audit trail runs two hundred years below his clearance. Then, the bible's line, verbatim, entire: **"I knew the system was imperfect. I didn't know it was designed to be."**
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:279:What he does next is the arc, staged precisely: he does not offer alliance, does not touch the exhibits again, does not ask where the cache lives — and does not reach for a pen. He tells them, in the register of a man reading his own evaluation aloud, what he can and cannot do: he cannot verify this through any channel that would not destroy them and him to no effect — every verification instrument the Compact owns runs on the thing being verified. He will not report it — and the chapter marks that sentence as the crossing it is: an Archmarshal, in knowing possession, filing nothing; the keeper pattern, at forty years' altitude. And he will no longer lend the machine his hands: the proceedings have asked him to certify the re-evaluation protocol's applicability to the enrollee — the trap's last wanted signature, the best evaluator's name to make the corridor look like an evaluation. "They will have to proceed without my name on it. It's the only sentence I have left that they still need." Asked directly — by Lira, who else — whether he's with them, his answer keeps the bible's line exact: "No. I am not with anyone. For forty years I was an instrument, and I have just learned what I was an instrument *of*. A man should sit with that before he chooses what he becomes next." He stands, thanks the room with unperformed courtesy — the series' third staging of the gesture — and is gone.
books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/CHAPTER_ARCHITECTURE.md:288:The proceedings conclude — the chapter where the system finishes its sentence, staged as procedure throughout, because the book's thesis is that the pretense never needs to drop *inside the room*: the room IS the pretense. The final sessions at Ostrand, compressed and braided: Jent's closing submission (discretion, integrity, the unscorable tournament exhibit deployed with courteous efficiency); the defense's closing (Karis's masterwork, entered in full, and the chapter says plainly it is the best legal argument the series has staged — better than the Greyvane hearing — and that it changes nothing, which is the point); and the refused signature landing in the record as one procedural line — *evaluation-protocol certification: declined by the evaluator of record; proceeding on the presiding seat's authority* — the Archmarshal's withheld name a silence every registry-literate person on the continent will read.

