23:*So, the etiquette, as observed and not as explained: walk quiet. Carry steel. Don't light anything you don't need. Don't declare. Nobody has given me the reason for any of it and I am not going to make one up. Claim: there is a reason. Evidence: three carters and a boy's hand knocked down. Ruling: hold.*
73:"You walk ahead of me," the carter said. "Not far. Quiet — no talk on the stretch. Nothing lit. Steel where you can reach it. And you don't declare. Not on that stretch. Not for anything."
77:"You don't declare."
107:From the lip of the wash, without turning his head toward the scrub, without raising his voice above the pitch of the road, Seln said: "Don't declare anything."

Potential place/proper names disallowed scan
25:*Ninth day. Lowmarch.*
29:*The board is under a roof on four posts by the ferry landing: planks nailed to the posts at head height, slips of paper pinned to the planks. I read it the way I read the Cinder House board at fourteen, which is to say all of it, twice, and then sorted.*
35:*Pike keeps the board. Sixties. One arm — the left sleeve pinned flat by someone who has done it every morning for years and stopped thinking about it. He sits the way Force practitioners sit when they have stopped using it, weight all the way down. He said "Copper, Force, thirty years on the roads" in the same breath as his name, as a man says his trade, and did not ask ours. He asked what we could do. Lira said "Walk, carry, climb, fight," and he said "In that order?" and she said "Depends on the day," and he wrote something down.*
41:*First contract. Ferry escort: a day on the ferry, six crossings, with a broker's bales and a broker who wanted six people visible on his goods. Nothing happened. Two marks for standing where we could be seen, which is a job I have done before, in a gallery, at a higher price and for worse people. Pike's line, read upside down: escort, ferry, six crossings, no loss, two marks. The broker made his mark under "witness." The line does not say who we are. It says what we did.*
57:The fourth contract came to their fire instead of the other way round, on the forty-first evening, in the shape of a man who had asked Pike where the cheap crew slept.
61:"Pike says you're six," the carter said, "and cheap."
65:"Thornwater. Grain, one cart, two days. Nine marks and the hold feeds you." He looked at the fire rather than at any of them. "There's a stretch on the second day. Road drops into the scrub for two miles before the fence. Two carts went quiet on it last month. One we found. Cart, mule, no driver. The other we didn't."
117:Forty meters. He knew forty meters as well as he knew his own stride; it was the long side of the Ironyard floor, and he had chalked it himself once, at fifteen.
151:Holding was never one act; it was a sequence of small refusals arriving faster than a person could name them. The Iron-adjacent read was the cheapest thing he owned and it wanted the ground under the thing's feet, wanted its weight and its gait through the surface of the valley, and he did not give it the ground. Anchor, which had been wanting fixed points in ordinary doorways since a road outside Ostrand, found the wash's near lip beside him and wanted a line laid along it, and he let it want. Shadow, which had been trying to switch itself on at dusk every evening since the Line, tried, and he held it off as he had held it off for three hours once in a room off a records hall, under the heaviest observation of his life. Wind sat where Wind sat. Storm sat where Storm sat, heavy. None of them moved.
185:Thornwater at the gate, catalogued while a woman with a lantern looked them over: a fence of split timber, close-set, twice a man's height, running left and right into the dark farther than the lamps reached; the gate two leaves of the same timber, barred, with a walk above it where a man stood with a bow and did not point it; behind the fence, roofs, four or five, low and thatched, and the smell of animals and a bake-oven and wet earth. A steading. The dog he had heard was on a chain inside and had stopped barking the moment the gate-woman spoke to it, which told him more about the place than the fence did.
203:"Stillhound," he said.
207:"It's a stillhound. You shut up. It went home." He took up the slack in the rein. "Twenty years. That's all I've got, and I've had it a while."
217:The carter had a slip. He took it from inside his coat, folded to the size of a palm, and gave it to the gate-woman, and she unfolded it against the gatepost and read it by her lantern — Pike's hand, the posting copied out, five ruled lines beneath it — and made her mark under the fourth, and said "Delivered. No losses," aloud, because that was plainly the procedure, and gave it back. It would go back to Lowmarch in his coat, and Pike would write what it said.
225:They stayed four days. The steading fed them for lofting the grain, which was not a contract and went on no board, and for a fence-post Brom reset because he could not walk past a leaning post. Lira ran the fence-line at first light each morning, because there was nothing at Thornwater to run at and she needed to run at something. Seln was gone for hours at a time and came back with the lay of every road out of the place, unasked, and once said "The wash goes round the north side" to Cael at supper as though it were the weather. Karis sat in the loft with the three boxes open around her and wrote, for most of one day, the single word the carter had given her at the gate, with a line under it, and beneath the line everything she had counted on the stretch, and beneath that nothing, because there was nothing beneath it yet.
229:Pike's line, copied in Pike's hand onto the back of the posting, because the crew could not be at the board to read it:
231:*Thornwater road, the stretch. Walked ahead of one cart, one carter, one mule. Delivered at the fence. No losses. Nine marks, paid.*
235:*Forty-sixth day. Thornwater. First roof since the Line.*
237:*Six weeks. Four contracts. Nobody has asked what I am. Nobody has asked what any of us are — the gate-woman asked if we were paper, and I said yes, and that was the end of her interest. The board asks what we did. Pike writes it down. The line about us is one line long and every word in it is true, and that is the whole system, and I have been looking for its other half for four days and there isn't one.*

