   541	  "purpose": "Open Book 7 by staging six weeks of hard-won, uninstrumented freedom in the edge territories — a country with no registry, no tier column, and a ledger that records only what you did — and then, in one dusk walk along a void road with a carter behind them and a silent grey shape pacing them, teach the reader the edge territories' first rule in the body before anyone explains it: out here, the banking doctrine four books built is not a legal skill but a survival one. The chapter must make the reader feel Arc 3's promise whole (Cael free for the first time) and end on a log entry that is confident, earned, and — the reader should half-sense — standing on something he cannot yet see.",
   542	  "scene_shape": {
   543	    "opening_state": "Log-voice opening movement, the series' established skip technique (B4/B5/B6 Ch1): six weeks beyond the Registry Line, dated and inventoried in Cael's plain first-person log register — the walk-in from the first camp; the last westbound courier turned back at the ford; void-road etiquette learned by watching carters; Lowmarch reached on the ninth day and its contract board read the way he read the Cinder House notice board at fourteen; the first three contracts (a ferry escort, a fence-line clearance, a cart recovered from a wash) taken and completed in summary — cheap, the unit finding its feet; Pike's ledger opened for them under a heading with no tier column. He is eighteen and says so on the page. Nine confirmed fragments. One anomaly. No file he can see.",
   544	    "pov_goal": "To do the fourth contract — the first with teeth — well enough to earn a line in the only ledger that exists out here, and to prove to himself that the six of them are a unit in a country that has never seen an academy or a circuit.",
   545	    "opposition": "The road itself and what lives beside it: a stretch of void road toward Thornwater where two carts went silent last month; dusk; a carter and a mule who must be walked through; and a grey shape in the scrub that makes no sound at all and keeps pace at roughly forty meters, never closing — waiting, though nobody says so, for someone to declare a Path.",
   546	    "turn": "Lira, twenty paces ahead, stops with her hand up — and the reason is the ABSENCE of a sound. Something is pacing them that has made no noise. Seln, without turning: 'Don't declare anything.' The unit, six practitioners with everything they own held in check, walks a mile of road with the shape keeping station. The temptation is real and specific: Lira's burst would clear it, Brom's Iron Skin would answer it, Karis could light the scrub — and every one of them holds, because the carters hold.",
   547	    "choice": "Cael chooses not to reach — not for any of the nine, not for the observation-turned-weapon — and instead runs the compound gaze at full depth on an ANIMAL for the first time in his life, cataloguing what it does and does not do (it keeps distance, it never crosses open ground, it tracks the mule and the six bodies differently, it reacts to nothing they do because they do nothing), and lets the not-doing be the whole tactic. The banking doctrine, inverted from law into survival.",
   548	    "outcome": "No fight. The shape peels off a hundred meters short of Thornwater's fence-line and is gone the way it came. At the steading's gate the carter, twenty years on this road, says: 'First crew I've hired that knew to shut up.' Cael files the sentence beside the best compliments of his life. Pike's line for the job, arriving by the carter's return, is the chapter's third-party verdict.",
   549	    "closing_state": "The Power Log, under a roof that isn't an institution's, honoring the card's close-on entry: six weeks, four contracts, nobody has asked what he is — the board asks what they DID, and Pike writes it down, and that is the whole system; something out here hunts Paths and he has been not-using his in front of hostile audiences since he was fifteen, which turns out to have been training; 'Note the date. Season's open, and for once it isn't theirs.' Confident, earned, and — for the reader — standing on ground the book will spend twenty-three chapters mapping."
