Book 2 age/Brom lines
     1	# CHAPTER ARCHITECTURE — Book 2: Iron Circuit
     2	**Canon status: PROVISIONAL**
     3	**Target: 110,000 words / 24 chapters / ~4,600 words per chapter**
     4	**Cael's age: 15–16 across this book**
     5	**Companions introduced: Brom (Iron Skin Path, Copper-tier)**
     6	**Arc: Survival → circuit legitimacy → first proof of self**
     7	
     8	---
   176	---
   177	
   178	**Chapter 8 — Brom**
   179	~4,600 words
   180	
   181	Brom's formal introduction. The chapter opens with a limited third-person perspective shift into Brom — brief, the same technique used for Coss's chapter in Book 1 — before returning to Cael.
   182	
   183	Brom is 16. He came to Ardenmere two months ago from his family's estate in Velmere, having walked out before they could formally stage the response to his Kindling result. Iron Skin Path, Copper-tier. His family expected Gold — the kind of expectation that produces either a practitioner who lives up to it or one who spends a decade being measured against something they weren't. Brom chose a third option: left before the measuring started. He's been doing circuit work in two other cities. He came to Ardenmere because its circuit is known in the practitioner network for accepting the most unusual competitors.
   184	
   185	He's been watching Cael for three weeks. What he observes: Cael's pattern recognition is extraordinary in a way that isn't explicable by experience level. His technique is heterodox — it doesn't match any Path framework Brom recognizes. Most importantly: Brom's Iron Skin Path includes a passive pressure-read capability, and when he uses it to read the practitioner signatures in a bout, Cael's signature is wrong. Not absent. Wrong. Like a Path operating outside the categories the pressure-read is designed for. This is extremely interesting.
   186	
   187	He finds Dace after watching Cael's latest bout. "I want to fight the unranked one." "He's Assessed now. Copper-equivalent." "I know." "He's been fighting Iron-equivalent opponents and winning." "I know." Dace reads Brom for a long moment. "You're Iron-equivalent yourself." "Yes." "Interesting." He schedules the bout for two weeks out.
   188	
   189	Brom informs Cael directly. He walks up to him after the session and says, without social preliminary: "Brom. Iron Skin Path. We fight two weeks from today." Cael, who clocked Brom as the stranger from Chapter 6 within thirty seconds of this meeting, says: "I know who you are." Brom, mildly surprised: "Do you." "You've been watching for three weeks." "You noticed." "Three weeks ago." A pause. Brom, recalibrating slightly: "What do you know about Iron Skin?" "Not enough." Brom, satisfied: "Good. Two weeks."
   190	

Draft finding lines
    10	
    11	*No tier. No rank. No enrollment. And — I checked this morning from the top of the shelf, both directions, the way I have checked every morning for four years — no file I can see.*
    12	
    13	*Fourth day. A ford.*
    14	
    15	*The last of the westbound couriers turned back here: a woman on a mule with a satchel of post, who took the two letters I have carried since the first camp and said this was as far as post went. Past the ford it is carters or nothing. She looked at the six of us and did not ask what we were. I waited for the question the whole time she was tightening her girth. It did not come. I am writing down that it did not come.*
    16	
    17	*Sixth day.*
    18	
    19	*Fell in behind three carts going east and watched them. The carters walk quiet. Not silent — quiet: no talk on the road, no singing, a word to the mule at need. Steel where it can be reached: a crossbow across the sacks, a bill-hook by the driver's knee. No fire until the road is behind them for the night, and then a small one. And when a boy on the second cart — sixteen, maybe — put his hand out toward a hillside as though he meant to try something, the driver knocked the hand down without looking round and said, "Not on the road."*
    20	
    21	*He did not say why. I asked at the fire. He said, "You're paper, aren't you," which is what they call anyone from inside the Line, and I said we were, and he said, "Then you know how to keep quiet. Keep it." I have had longer conversations with clerks and learned less.*
    22	
    23	*So, the etiquette, as observed and not as explained: walk quiet. Carry steel. Don't light anything you don't need. Don't declare. Nobody has given me the reason for any of it and I am not going to make one up. Claim: there is a reason. Evidence: three carters and a boy's hand knocked down. Ruling: hold.*
    24	
    25	*Ninth day. Lowmarch.*
    26	
    27	*A river, wide and brown, with a ford marked by poles and a rope ferry for the deep part. Two inns, priced for people with more coin than we have. A smith, working. No wall. A meadow above the ford full of carts and tents, which is where anyone who is not a carter sleeps, and where we are. The ferryman told Brom that half the faces in the meadow will be gone by the month's end and the other half will be new. Nobody here has been here long enough to be anybody.*
    28	
    29	*The board is under a roof on four posts by the ferry landing: planks nailed to the posts at head height, slips of paper pinned to the planks. I read it the way I read the Cinder House board at fourteen, which is to say all of it, twice, and then sorted.*
    30	
    31	*Fourteen postings. Six escorts. Three clearances — two fence-lines, one ford. Two recoveries — a cart in a wash, a load off a bank. Three hold contracts, priced high, with a word on each in the board-keeper's hand: HOLD, meaning a crew is on it. Every slip prices the job. Not one slip prices the crew. There is no line on any of them for what a crew has to be — no "Iron or above," no "certified," nothing — and I looked for it the way you look for a stair in the dark, with a foot, because every board I have ever read had it.*
    32	
    33	*Sorted by what each would cost the six of us: the escorts cost time. The fence-lines cost Brom's back. The wash costs rope and Lira. The ford clearance I can't price, because I don't know what is in the ford.*
    34	
    35	*Pike keeps the board. Sixties. One arm — the left sleeve pinned flat by someone who has done it every morning for years and stopped thinking about it. He sits the way Force practitioners sit when they have stopped using it, weight all the way down. He said "Copper, Force, thirty years on the roads" in the same breath as his name, as a man says his trade, and did not ask ours. He asked what we could do. Lira said "Walk, carry, climb, fight," and he said "In that order?" and she said "Depends on the day," and he wrote something down.*
    36	
    37	*He opened a heading for us. Six names down the margin of a fresh page, in his hand, spelled the way we said them. Five columns ruled beside the names: Job. Hirer. Witness. Outcome. Price. I read it upside down, because I read everything upside down, and then read it again to be sure I had not missed the column every ledger I have ever been entered in has had, the one that says what you are. I had not missed it. The page does not have it.*
    38	
    39	*Fourteenth day.*
    40	
    41	*First contract. Ferry escort: a day on the ferry, six crossings, with a broker's bales and a broker who wanted six people visible on his goods. Nothing happened. Two marks for standing where we could be seen, which is a job I have done before, in a gallery, at a higher price and for worse people. Pike's line, read upside down: escort, ferry, six crossings, no loss, two marks. The broker made his mark under "witness." The line does not say who we are. It says what we did.*
    42	
    92	
    93	The order had settled itself on the first day without a word from anyone, because four years of walking into rooms together had made it a habit. Lira went first, twenty paces ahead, on the balls of her feet. Cael and Karis came next, five paces ahead of the mule, on the crown of the road. Brom walked at the mule's near shoulder with one hand on the cheek-strap, because the carter had asked for a big man at the mule's head and had not said why, and because the mule had decided about Brom on the first morning. The carter walked at the off side with the rein. Seln was on the left, off the road, along the wash's lip, in the place four years had taught Cael to look for him last.
    94	
    95	The valley was making noise. He noticed that only afterward, when it stopped — the small continuous racket of a scrubland at dusk, insects, something dry moving in the thorn, a bird somewhere on the right saying the same two notes at intervals. The mule's harness had its own noise, a leather creak and a buckle that had not been set, which Cael had stopped trying to stop hearing.
    96	
    97	They had gone perhaps half a mile into the stretch when Lira stopped and put her hand up.
    98	
    99	She did not turn. Her hand went up open, palm back, the way it went up on a floor when she wanted a drill halted and did not want to spend breath on it, and she stood where she had stopped with her weight forward and did not move anything else.
   100	
   101	Brom stopped the mule. The carter said nothing. Karis, beside Cael, went still in the way that meant something had happened and she did not yet know what.
   102	
   103	Cael listened for it, and did not hear it, and then understood that this was the point.
   104	
   105	The right-hand side of the valley had gone silent. Not the whole valley — the wash side still ticked and whirred, the bird was still there somewhere behind them — but from the road to the valley wall on the right, for as far ahead as he could judge, the scrub had stopped making any sound at all. It was the shape of the silence Lira had heard. A hole in the noise, and the hole was keeping pace with them.
   106	
   107	From the lip of the wash, without turning his head toward the scrub, without raising his voice above the pitch of the road, Seln said: "Don't declare anything."
   108	
   109	Nobody answered him. Nobody needed to.
   110	
   111	"Walk," Seln said. "Same pace."
   112	
   113	Lira's hand came down. She walked. The mule walked, because Brom did. Cael walked, and did the thing he had trained himself to do on floors where the other man had already moved and he had not yet seen how, which was to catalogue instead of react.
   114	
   115	He found it on the second look. Forty meters off the road on the right, in the chest-high scrub, a place where the grey was a different grey — lower than the thorn, longer than a dog, so exactly the valley's colour that he lost it the moment he looked straight at it and found it again only at the edge of his eye. It was moving. It was moving when they moved, at their pace, and when he watched it for fifty paces it neither gained a stride nor lost one.
   116	
   117	Forty meters. He knew forty meters as well as he knew his own stride; it was the long side of the Ironyard floor, and he had chalked it himself once, at fifteen.
   118	
   119	Claim: it is alone. Evidence: one shape; one hole in the noise, no wider than one animal makes. Ruling: unproven. He looked left, at the wash side, for a second hole, and did not find one, and did not let that become a finding.
   120	
   121	He walked.
   122	
   123	Lira, twenty paces ahead, was walking the way she walked on a floor in the last minute before a bout: everything in her wanting to go, nothing in her going. He knew what she could do to it. One burst — Wind, her own, Iron Rank One by an instrument's account — and the forty meters of scrub between her and it would lie flat, and the thing would either run or be found. She had said as much to nobody in the set of her shoulders. Then she said it in words, low, not turning.
   124	
   125	"I could clear it."
   126	
   127	"No," said Seln.
   128	
   129	"I know. I'm saying it so I've said it."
   130	
   131	Nobody said anything after that for two hundred paces.
   132	
   133	Brom's hand had not moved on the mule's cheek-strap. Cael had watched Brom answer anything with teeth for six years, and the answer had always been the same: to become the thing teeth broke on. He was not becoming it. He was walking at a mule's shoulder in the near dark with his shoulders down and his breathing slow, exactly as large as his body made him and not one degree larger, and once, when the buckle jingled, he said "Easy" to the mule in a voice that carried nowhere at all.
   134	
   135	"Mule's fine," he said, a little later, to the carter, at the same pitch.
   136	
   137	The carter had a crossbow across the sacks and had not touched it.
   138	
   139	Karis was the one Cael watched. She had never seen a large animal she did not want to document. She walked with her right hand at her coat, where the notebook lived, and Cael saw her decide not to take it out four separate times in a hundred paces. She could have lit the scrub. One point of ignition at contact, one channel, and the whole right side of the valley would have been a wall the thing had to go round, and she would have had a sample by its light. Her hand went to the coat a fifth time and stopped there.
